## Challenges and Troubleshooting

During the initial setup, the external LED remained OFF even when the push button was pressed.

To identify the problem, I tested different parts of the circuit individually.

### 1. Testing the Push Button

I used a digital multimeter in continuity mode to identify the correct contacts of the four-legged push button.

I observed that some pairs of legs were permanently connected, while the switched contacts showed continuity only when the button was pressed.

This helped me understand the internal connections of a tactile push button.

### 2. Verifying Breadboard Connections

I tested the button through the breadboard using the multimeter.

The continuity test confirmed that the button circuit was open when released and closed when pressed.

This helped me understand how breadboard strips are electrically connected.

### 3. Testing the ESP32

I uploaded a basic blinking program using GPIO 2 to verify that the ESP32 was executing the code.

The onboard blue LED blinked successfully.

### 4. Testing GPIO 23

I measured GPIO output voltage using a multimeter and investigated unexpected readings.

I then uploaded an LED blinking program using GPIO 23.

The external red LED blinked successfully, confirming that the LED, resistor and GPIO 23 output circuit were working.

### 5. Correcting the Button Circuit

After verifying the LED circuit, I reconnected the push button to GPIO 18 and GND.

The final circuit worked correctly, turning the LED ON when pressed and OFF when released.

**Likely issue:** An incorrect or incomplete connection in the button-to-GND path during the initial assembly. The exact faulty connection was not conclusively identified.

**Resolution:** Verified each part of the circuit independently and corrected the wiring before reconnecting the complete system.

